---
import {
  MCP_RELEASE,
  NOMUE_MCP_CLIENT_CONFIG,
  NOMUE_MCP_NPX_COMMAND,
  NOMUE_MCP_REPLAY_COMMAND,
  NOMUE_MCP_WHEN_NOT_TO_USE,
  NOMUE_MCP_WHEN_TO_USE,
} from '../../data/site-facts';
import PublicationLayout from '../../layouts/PublicationLayout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'available', label: 'What is available' },
  { id: 'configure', label: 'Add it to a client' },
  { id: 'tool', label: 'Tool boundary' },
  { id: 'replay', label: 'Independent replay' },
  { id: 'local-boundary', label: 'Local boundary' },
  { id: 'release-status', label: 'Release status' },
];
---

<PublicationLayout
  category="News"
  type="Product"
  title="nomue local MCP release candidate is available on npm"
  description="The public @licklider/nomue-mcp package lets MCP clients call the exact Release 1 Welch Record verifier over local stdio."
  published="September 2, 2026"
  status="Public local MCP release candidate — npm rc 0.1.0-rc.0; official MCP Registry"
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="available">
    <h2>What is available</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={MCP_RELEASE.npmUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>{MCP_RELEASE.package}@{MCP_RELEASE.version}</code></a>
      {' '}is publicly available on npm and registered as{' '}
      <a href={MCP_RELEASE.registryUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>{MCP_RELEASE.registryName}</code></a>
      {' '}in the official MCP Registry. It is a local stdio server with one tool,
      <code>{MCP_RELEASE.tool}</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The server is a thin adapter around the exact{' '}
      <code>{MCP_RELEASE.verifierPackage}@{MCP_RELEASE.verifierVersion}</code> npm
      package. It does not reimplement statistical or Protocol semantics and does not
      add a blanket verification verdict.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="configure">
    <h2>Add it to an MCP client</h2>
    <pre><code>{NOMUE_MCP_CLIENT_CONFIG}</code></pre>
    <p>Or start the same exact package directly:</p>
    <pre><code>{NOMUE_MCP_NPX_COMMAND}</code></pre>
    <p>
      The first <code>npx</code> launch may download npm dependencies. No account, API
      key, environment variable, or Licklider-hosted URL is required. Client
      configurations pin the exact release-candidate version rather than relying on a
      mutable npm tag.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="tool">
    <h2>One bounded Welch Record tool</h2>
    <p>{NOMUE_MCP_WHEN_TO_USE}</p>
    <p>{NOMUE_MCP_WHEN_NOT_TO_USE}</p>
    <pre><code>{`{"record_json":"{...complete nomue Record JSON text...}"}`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      <code>record_json</code> contains the complete Record as JSON text, not a parsed
      object. Preserving the original text keeps strict parsing, duplicate-member
      rejection, digest checks, and resource limits inside the verifier.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first MCP text block preserves the verifier stdout from that invocation.
      Structured content carries the same parsed verifier artifact, while metadata
      preserves the verifier exit code and input/output digests. Transport success does
      not mean that every scoped check passed, and passing scoped checks do not establish
      overall scientific validity.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="replay">
    <h2>Replay independently with the npm verifier</h2>
    <p>Save the same Record JSON text as <code>record.json</code>, then run:</p>
    <pre><code>{NOMUE_MCP_REPLAY_COMMAND}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Every substantive artifact field is independently reproducible. Separate verifier
      invocations intentionally create different top-level <code>generated_at</code>
      timestamps, so cross-invocation byte identity is not claimed. Byte preservation is
      exact within the MCP invocation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="local-boundary">
    <h2>Local-only Phase 1 boundary</h2>
    <p>
      After npm installs the package and its dependencies, verification runs locally and
      does not call a Licklider service. Release CI tests the stdio package path on
      Linux, macOS, and Windows with Node.js 20 and 22. It does not expose HTTP,
      authentication, logging, rate limiting, an SLA, or a management interface.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="release-status">
    <h2>Release status and exact scope</h2>
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        This is version <code>{MCP_RELEASE.version}</code>, published through the npm{' '}
        <code>{MCP_RELEASE.npmDistTag}</code> channel. npm also created
        <code>latest</code> on this first package publication; that registry behavior is
        not a stable-release claim. The exact version remains pinned in every client
        example.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      The release candidate does not expand the Protocol support target. It verifies
      existing nomue Release 1 Welch Records only. Paired-t, Wilcoxon, Mann–Whitney,
      raw-data calculation, method selection, source-data truth, causality, and overall
      scientific correctness remain outside this tool's result.
    </p>
    <p>
      See the <a href="/docs/mcp-verification/">MCP installation and result contract</a>{' '}
      for the complete machine-readable boundary and replay instructions.
    </p>
  </section>
</PublicationLayout>
